          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What counts as insomnia rather than just a few bad nights?",
          "answer": "The main difference is how long the problem lasts and whether it affects your day.\n\nUnder the \"Insomnia\" educational summary, insomnia has two parts, and both must be present. The first is trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early, even when you have enough time to sleep. The second is daytime effects from that poor sleep. Sleeping six hours and feeling fine is not insomnia. Sleeping six hours and being impaired by it may be.\n\nThe same source describes two patterns:\n- **Short-term insomnia** lasts days to weeks, usually has a clear trigger, and often gets better on its own.\n- **Chronic insomnia disorder** is defined as problems on at least three nights a week for at least three months. It can continue after the original trigger has gone.\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover how clinicians assess or treat insomnia, so I can't say what steps to take next. If your sleep problems have lasted several weeks or are affecting your work, driving, or mood, it would be reasonable to raise them with a clinician.\n\nSource: condition-insomnia.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
